WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.210
Hello everyone, welcome to the LangFocus Channel and my name is Paul. Today, we'll be discussing the question,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.760
"How similar are Metropolitan French and Quebec French or Québécois?"

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.880
In other words, "how similar is the French spoken in France to the French spoken in Quebec?" French is one of the world's major

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:19.289
languages and it's spoken in dozens of different

00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:26.039
countries and everywhere that it's spoken the standard variety or the formal variety that's learned at school and used in the media is

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.000
essentially the same as metropolitan French based on the Parisian variety of French.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.120
In informal speech however, there is some variation with local differences in accent and vocabulary.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:41.279
There's one major variety of French that is particularly distinct from Metropolitan French

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:49.200
And that is the French of Quebec, a culturally distinct French-speaking province in Canada. French speakers from France often say that they have trouble understanding

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:51.000
the French spoken in Quebec.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.770
I should point out that the formal variety of French used in Quebec is virtually identical to

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.760
metropolitan French. The differences lie in the informal varieties of Quebec and in informal speech there can be

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.780
a considerable amount of difference. But why is Quebec French distinct from metropolitan French?

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.959
The reason lies in the settlement of New France and the later

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:15.989
isolation of Quebec from France. New France was an area in North America that was colonized by France

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:22.259
before the creation of Canada and the United States. The first settlers in New France were not mainly from the Paris area

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:26.879
but from regions like Normandy and Brittany where regional sister languages of French,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.150
langues d'oïl, were spoken rather than French itself.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.328
But since French settlers were outnumbered by English settlers,

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:34.509
the King of France,

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:41.459
King Louis XIV, sent female settlers, mainly from the Paris area to help bolster their numbers through marriage and childbirth.

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:47.188
Since these women were mainly from the Paris area they brought with them their parisian variety of French, which

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:52.258
contributed to the decline of the regional languages and the adoption of French among the settler population.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:56.549
And of course the interaction between speakers of different regional languages

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:03.599
encouraged the use of French as a lingua franca. As a result, the language that developed in New France was similar to the language spoken

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.290
by the King and the aristocracy,

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:12.959
but was also influenced by the regional languages of the settlers. The French language became adopted throughout the various regions of France LATER,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.140
after the French Revolution of the late 18th century.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.020
The variety of French that became the new widespread standard language was not the French of the King and the aristocracy,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.260
but rather the French of the bourgeois class who led the revolution.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.720
Prior to the French Revolution, New France had already been conquered by the British in 1760

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.780
and its elite had returned to France. The British soon decided to allow the

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:43.180
French-speaking population in what is now Quebec to continue using their language as a way to prevent them from joining the American Revolution

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.080
against the British, which was taking place to the south. Quebec therefore remained under British rule

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:54.930
but was isolated from France. The French language in Quebec began developing in its own direction while the French spoken in France

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.099
developed in its own direction. VOCABULARY. The vocabulary of Quebec French differs from that of metropolitan French in several ways.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.559
First, the two varieties of French have taken in loanwords from English during different time periods.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.460
The French of Quebec was infused with English vocabulary during the period from

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:16.350
1850 to 1960, for example, the word for job. In Quebec French,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:23.460
there's "une job", in metropolitan French, "un travail"/"un emploi". In Quebec French, "weird" and

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.400
metropolitan French, "bizarre".

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.260
In Quebec French, there's "chum" meaning a male friend or boyfriend and in metropolitan French,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:38.260
that would be "mon petit ami" or "un copain". In Quebec French, there's "fun".

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.800
In Metropolitan French, it's "amusant"

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.999
"Fun" also appears in the phrase "avoir du fun",

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:51.510
which is a direct translation of the English expression "to have fun". In Metropolitan French, it would be "s'amuser".

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:55.560
Expressions like this are called "calques" or loan translations.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:02.369
Another example is "ça fait du sens". This is translated from English, "that makes sense". In Metropolitan French,

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:08.850
it would be "ça a du sens". And in Quebec French people say "bienvenue" to mean "you're welcome".

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.480
But in Metropolitan French they would say, "de rien". "Bienvenue" means "welcome" in all varieties of French,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.059
but not "you're welcome" in response to "thank you".

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:26.829
Note that these are informal québécois words, which are not considered proper in formal Quebec French, which mirrors the standards of Metropolitan French.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:34.269
Since 1960, there have been French Language protection laws in Quebec and a general distaste towards anglicisms.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:41.259
Metropolitan French, on the other hand, doesn't have the same protective attitude and in recent decades has been more influenced by English,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:46.480
so it contains recent English loanwords that are not used in Quebec. For example,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.540
in Metropolitan French they say "email" but in Quebec French they say "un courriel".

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:59.500
In Metropolitan French, there's "smartphone". In Quebec, they say "téléphone intelligent". Stop signs in France

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.559
say "STOP". This is not really a part of the French language,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.450
it's just an internationally used street sign, but in Quebec the signs say "ARRÊT".

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:15.369
Another aspect of vocabulary that differs is that there are some words or expressions in Quebec French that seem older or more traditional

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:23.470
to speakers of Metropolitan French. The québécois word for shoes is "des souliers", in metropolitan French, it's "des chaussures".

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:29.800
The québécois word for car, "un char". In France, it's "une voiture."

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.040
In France "char" means tank, as in, tank of war.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.480
Another example is the way to say "I miss you" in Quebec.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.837
It's "je m'ennuie de vous". In France,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.480
that would sound rather old-fashioned and the way people would usually say it is "vous me manquez."

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:52.460
The French of Quebec also contains numerous slang expressions and swear words that are taken from the Catholic religion.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:59.619
Perhaps the most famous swear word of Quebec is "tabernak". Others include "câlice" meaning chalice,

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.950
"osti" meaning host or communion bread.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:08.289
These words are only offensive in Quebec and nowhere else in the francophone world, as far as I know,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:16.600
and the French of Quebec has also just developed some of its own vocabulary and expressions. For example, to go shopping in Quebec French its "faire du magasinage",

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:21.880
in Metropolitan French, it's "faire des courses"/"faire du shopping."

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:27.880
GRAMMAR. Written French and Quebec is grammatically identical to Metropolitan French

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:31.400
but in informal speech you might hear some differences in grammar.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.980
"Tu" is used after verbs as a yes-or-no question marker. "T'as-tu une blonde?"

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:39.979
This means "Do you have a girlfriend?" In metropolitan French, it's

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:46.729
"t'as une petite amie?" Notice here that "tu" is being used as a yes-or-no question marker and not as a pronoun.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.120
Also notice the québécois word for girlfriend, "blonde".

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.889
"C'est-tu prêt?" this means "Is it ready?" In Metropolitan French,

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:56.789
that would be "C'est prêt?"

00:06:56.789 --> 00:07:03.559
"Est-ce que c'est prêt?" Notice that the subject is third person. To be clear, "tu" is nothing but a question marker here.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.630
It's not the second person pronoun "tu."

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.509
It's also used for emphasis or exclamation "c'est-tu choquant!" - "that's so shocking".

00:07:12.509 --> 00:07:14.190
"C'est-si choquant!"

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:20.389
Next, in Quebec French, "que" is used as the relative pronoun more often than in Metropolitan French.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:26.000
"Je cherche le livre que j'ai besoin". This means "I'm looking for the book that I need".

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.440
In Metropolitan French, this would be "Je cherche le livre dont j'ai besoin".

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.489
In a sentence with an embedded question, in Metropolitan French you use a relative pronoun, but in Quebec French

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.849
the interrogative pronoun is used. "Je fais qu'est ce que je veux."

00:07:41.099 --> 00:07:45.679
This means "I do what I want." In Metropolitan French, "je fais ce que je veux."

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.870
Next, in Quebec French,

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:55.070
the explicit subject of a sentence is often said as a tag, with a pronoun as the subject in the main clause.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:01.340
For example "Mon boss, y me met tellement en colère," This means "My boss makes me so angry."

00:08:02.009 --> 00:08:07.908
Notice how the subject my boss is set aside at the beginning, separate from the main clause, and then the pronoun "y"

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:13.309
which is a contraction of "il" appears in the main clause. In metropolitan French this would be,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.830
"Mon patron me met tellement en colère."

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.359
In Quebec French, "Son plan, c'est de voyager autour du monde."

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:27.900
This means "his plan is to travel around the world." In Metropolitan French. It would be, "Son projet est de voyager autour du monde."

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:33.960
This type of tag is also used in Metropolitan French but is much more common in Quebec French.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:39.080
Next, there are some differences in imperative forms in Quebec French, "Dis-moé le!"

00:08:39.269 --> 00:08:42.199
This means "tell me" or "tell it to me".

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:48.739
In Metropolitan French, "Dis-le-moi!" You can see that the object pronoun "le" is positioned differently.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:54.919
Now the negative form, "Dis-moé le pas!" In metropolitan French "Ne me le dis pas!"

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:58.820
You can see that in the negative, the syntax is quite different.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:01.230
In Metropolitan French, the object pronoun

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.700
"me" is used instead of the French emphatic pronoun "moi",

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:12.529
often pronounced "moé" in Quebec and it comes before the verb as does the object pronoun "le". In Quebec French, they come after the verb,

00:09:12.529 --> 00:09:14.529
as they do in the affirmative.

00:09:15.329 --> 00:09:23.299
accent probably the biggest difference between the French of Quebec and metropolitan France simply lies in accent without previous exposure to

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:29.839
The French spoken in Quebec French speakers from Europe typically have trouble understanding it and this differs depending on the socio-economic

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:36.560
background and the region of the speaker the consonants are not so different for the most part, but there are some differences in

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:43.250
Quebec French before the vowels E and E the consonants to end become

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:45.149
Anza

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:46.320
gg

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.339
Whereas in metropolitan French it's pronounced 2d

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:57.890
There are several different Rohtak sounds used throughout Quebec including trill sounds as opposed to the uvular fricative of metropolitan French

00:09:58.079 --> 00:09:58.579
however

00:09:58.579 --> 00:10:04.369
This is also true in France where some regional accents have trills rather than fricatives and in Quebec

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.690
informal speech and in many areas

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.019
The uvular fricative is also used

00:10:09.149 --> 00:10:15.979
So the situation in Quebec and France is similar except that in France the shift towards the uvular fricative is more advanced

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:21.919
Vowels one difference is that nasal vowels are quite different in Quebec French

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.310
There's cat and in metropolitan French, there's cone

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:32.539
Another difference is that in Quebec French long vowels and nasal vowels come diphthongs in open syllables

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:39.230
So in Quebec French we have fight but in metropolitan French, there's fit in Quebec French

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:41.230
We have pie in

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.550
Metropolitan French it's damn

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:52.009
related to that Quebec French retains some long vowels from older French that have become short in metropolitan French for example in

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.730
Metropolitan French these two words sound the same MIT

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.879
MIT but in Quebec French they don't

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.550
Miette knit

00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:06.198
These are just a couple of examples but there are numerous distinctions like these in vowels

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.639
some words also become contracted in Quebec French, for example

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.199
Su meaning on followed by a definite article gets contracted

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.519
soon

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:18.930
sir

